WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.933
Startled by the sound of gunfire, the soldiers at Fort Kearney hurried to the train. The Indians

2
00:00:05.934 --> 00:00:10.826
quickly gave up the fight and fled. As the Indians galloped away, the

3
00:00:10.827 --> 00:00:19.229
passengers gathered on the station platform. Some of the passengers were wounded, but Aouda was safe and unharmed. Phileas Fogg had not received

4
00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:23.510
a scratch, and Fix was only slightly wounded on his arm. They had

5
00:00:23.511 --> 00:00:27.574
all been lucky. Colonel Proctor was the person most gravely injured, having been shot

6
00:00:27.575 --> 00:00:31.673
through the thigh. It was soon discovered, however, that three passengers were missing,

7
00:00:31.674 --> 00:00:36.057
including the brave Frenchman, Passepartout. The passengers looked around. The clouds were low

8
00:00:36.058 --> 00:00:40.279
and snow had begun to fall in earnest. The windows

9
00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.604
of the train were broken, and blood stained the dirt

10
00:00:43.605 --> 00:00:49.249
around the tracks. Aouda's face was wet with tears. She did not know if Passepartout was dead or

11
00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:55.075
alive. Mr. Fogg, with folded arms, remained motionless. He had a serious decision to make. Aouda looked at

12
00:00:55.076 --> 00:00:58.450
him without speaking, and he understood her look. "I

13
00:00:58.451 --> 00:01:03.973
will find him, living or dead," he told her quietly. "Ah, Mr. Fogg!" Aouda cried, holding his

14
00:01:03.974 --> 00:01:09.375
hands. "I hope it will be living," he added. "But we must not lose a

15
00:01:09.376 --> 00:01:13.188
moment." With this resolution, Phileas Fogg had decided to

16
00:01:13.189 --> 00:01:16.503
sacrifice his fortune. The delay of a single day would

17
00:01:16.504 --> 00:01:20.275
make him miss the steamer at New York and lose his wager. But

18
00:01:20.276 --> 00:01:27.275
his thought was, "It is my duty." Mr. Fogg approached the commanding officer of the fort and said, "Sir, three passengers have disappeared."

19
00:01:27.276 --> 00:01:32.401
"Dead?" asked the captain. "Dead or prisoners, it is uncertain. Do you plan to

20
00:01:32.402 --> 00:01:40.637
pursue the Indians? The lives of three men are in question," said Mr. Fogg. "Can I risk the lives of fifty men to save three?"

21
00:01:40.638 --> 00:01:43.498
the officer asked. "I don't know whether

22
00:01:43.499 --> 00:01:47.674
you can, sir, but you should!" said Mr. Fogg. "Nobody here has

23
00:01:47.675 --> 00:01:51.858
the right to teach me my duty!" said the captain. "Fine," said

24
00:01:51.859 --> 00:01:56.937
Mr. Fogg. "Then I will go alone." Upon hearing this, Fix exclaimed, "You, sir! All alone

25
00:01:56.938 --> 00:02:00.247
in pursuit of the Indians?" "I will not leave

26
00:02:00.248 --> 00:02:03.660
these men to die. I will go," said Mr. Fogg. "You are

27
00:02:03.661 --> 00:02:07.307
a brave man, sir," declared the captain. "You will not go

28
00:02:07.308 --> 00:02:10.562
alone." Turning to his soldiers, he

29
00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:12.670
asked for thirty

30
00:02:12.671 --> 00:02:16.475
volunteers. The whole company stepped forward at once. The captain selected

31
00:02:16.476 --> 00:02:19.657
thirty and chose an experienced sergeant to

32
00:02:19.658 --> 00:02:23.818
lead them. Mr. Fogg then

33
00:02:23.819 --> 00:02:30.038
turned to Fix and said, "I wish to ask you a favor. Please remain with

34
00:02:30.039 --> 00:02:36.638
Aouda until I return. In case anything should happen to me . . ." Fix's face went pale. Separate himself from the man he

35
00:02:36.639 --> 00:02:41.358
had followed so closely for so long? Let Mr. Fogg wander the desert chasing Indians? How could

36
00:02:41.359 --> 00:02:46.583
he? Fix looked at Mr. Fogg. The gentleman's gaze was calm and frank. Fix

37
00:02:46.584 --> 00:02:53.300
lowered his eyes and said, "I will stay." A few moments later, Mr. Fogg pressed Aouda's hand. He handed her his precious carpetbag

38
00:02:53.301 --> 00:03:00.342
and went off with the sergeant and his squad to mount their horses. Just before they rode off,

39
00:03:00.343 --> 00:03:06.207
Mr. Fogg faced the soldiers and said, "My friends, I will divide five-thousand dollars

40
00:03:06.208 --> 00:03:09.196
among you if we save the prisoners." It was

41
00:03:09.197 --> 00:03:18.216
then a little past noon. Aouda retired to the waiting room, where she sat alone, thinking of the generosity and tranquil courage of Phileas Fogg. Fix did not have the same

42
00:03:18.217 --> 00:03:21.130
thoughts, however, and could scarcely conceal his

43
00:03:21.131 --> 00:03:25.003
agitation. "I have been an idiot—an idiot

44
00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:29.627
to let him go alone! I have let him out

45
00:03:29.628 --> 00:03:36.606
of my sight!" he said to himself. He walked feverishly up and down the platform, but after a while

46
00:03:36.607 --> 00:03:40.514
he regained his outward composure. The hours crept by slowly. Aouda was

47
00:03:40.515 --> 00:03:44.906
paralyzed with the fear that Mr. Fogg would never return and that

48
00:03:44.907 --> 00:03:52.553
she would be alone in the world once again. Toward two o'clock that afternoon, long whistles were heard approaching from

49
00:03:52.554 --> 00:03:57.138
the east. It was the train's runaway engine, which had continued down the rails until

50
00:03:57.139 --> 00:04:00.938
the engineer regained consciousness and reversed its course. Most of the

51
00:04:00.939 --> 00:04:05.667
passengers were happy to see the engine resume its place at the head of the train. Now

52
00:04:05.668 --> 00:04:09.982
they could continue their journey. But Aouda desperately pleaded with the

53
00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:13.074
engineer to wait for Mr. Fogg

54
00:04:13.075 --> 00:04:19.371
and the other missing passengers. "I cannot interrupt the trip," replied the engineer. "We are already three hours behind." "And when will another

55
00:04:19.372 --> 00:04:24.309
train pass here from San Francisco?" she asked. "Tomorrow evening, madam." "Well then, I will wait," said

56
00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:34.386
Aouda. "I will remain with her," said Mr. Fix. "I will make sure no harm comes to her while we

57
00:04:34.387 --> 00:04:40.480
wait." The engineer then boarded the train, blew a farewell whistle, and the train slowly moved off across the snowy

58
00:04:40.481 --> 00:04:44.476
plain. Passepartout's Guidebook Didn't anybody hear me the first time? I'm

59
00:04:44.477 --> 00:04:48.348
still here, captured by Indians. Okay, I'll yell louder. Help! Help! Somebody, help me!

60
00:04:48.349 --> 00:04:53.057
The Indians startled me, but it's not at all surprising that our train was

61
00:04:53.058 --> 00:04:59.238
attacked because we are traveling across America during the Indian Wars. These are violent conflicts between Native American tribes and

62
00:04:59.239 --> 00:05:04.305
American settlers. I am told that the Indian Wars are occurring because white Americans have taken over Native

63
00:05:04.306 --> 00:05:12.295
American land and broken special agreements called treaties. Many Native American leaders, or chiefs, are becoming known for their bravery and intelligence. I have heard stories about

64
00:05:12.296 --> 00:05:15.765
two chiefs in particular—Sitting Bull and Red Cloud—who are leading

65
00:05:15.766 --> 00:05:19.100
efforts against the white men. We'll see how these wars turn

66
00:05:19.101 --> 00:05:29.865
out . . . I hope the violence ends soon. In the meantime . . . Help me!

